WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.050
Around 40 million.

00:00:03.130 --> 00:00:06.060
That's the number of people affected by cancer worldwide.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:11.180
Cancer arises when DNA mutations cause a cell to divide uncontrollably and 

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.420
behave abnormally.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.610
So even though the cancer cell starts out as a normal cell, because it accumulates 

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.020
mutations over time,

00:00:19.030 --> 00:00:21.970
it transforms into something that looks foreign.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.220
Our bodies already have a system in place,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:29.820
the immune system, whose job it is to look for and kill foreign invaders.

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:32.110
The usual invaders are bacteria,

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.550
viruses and parasites,

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:35.670
but researchers started to think,

00:00:35.680 --> 00:00:40.130
what if we could engage our immune system to not just search out pathogens,

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:44.340
but to also look for and kill the foreign looking cancer cells?

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:48.040
This idea sparked a revolution in cancer research,

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:53.260
the growing field of cancer immunotherapy. Using the immune system to kill 

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.770
cancer cells has become a very exciting and promising treatment strategy for cancer.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.450
So how exactly do cancer immunotherapies work?

00:01:02.470 --> 00:01:03.350
To understand this,

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:08.150
we need to understand how the cells of our immune system look for and then kill foreign 

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:08.850
invaders.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.990
The main killing cells of our immune system are called T-cells.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:16.710
These immune cells are constantly moving around our bodies,

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:21.740
and they make contacts with other cells they encounter to see whether these cells look 

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:23.960
normal or if they look foreign.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:29.910
Immune cells do this by using molecules on their surface that recognize proteins 

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:31.190
on the other cells.

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:35.490
If the T-cell makes contact and recognizes something foreign,

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.380
like a virus infected cell,

00:01:37.530 --> 00:01:40.350
it gets a green light for going on the attack,

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:45.300
and the immune cell will kill the infected cell. To make sure that our immune cells 

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.230
don't get out of control and damage healthy cells,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.700
some molecules on the T-cells act as brakes,

00:01:51.710 --> 00:01:55.870
providing a counteracting force that slows the immune attack 

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:56.780
down.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:01:57.830
These molecules,

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:01.000
which tell the immune cells whether to stop or go,

00:02:01.145 --> 00:02:03.025
are called immune checkpoints.

00:02:03.545 --> 00:02:06.385
So what does this have to do with cancer treatment?

00:02:06.395 --> 00:02:06.965
Well,

00:02:06.975 --> 00:02:11.475
cancer cells look a bit like foreign cells because they have lots of DNA 

00:02:11.475 --> 00:02:12.255
changes,

00:02:12.615 --> 00:02:14.845
some of which result in altered proteins.

00:02:15.045 --> 00:02:15.745
However,

00:02:15.755 --> 00:02:17.215
cancer cells are sneaky,

00:02:17.225 --> 00:02:21.505
and in order to avoid triggering a green light attack response from the 

00:02:21.725 --> 00:02:22.255
T-cells,

00:02:22.445 --> 00:02:25.505
they make proteins that elicit the red light response.

00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:26.705
By doing this,

00:02:26.925 --> 00:02:31.755
the cancer cells trick the immune cells into stepping on the brakes instead of on the 

00:02:31.755 --> 00:02:32.855
accelerator pedal.

00:02:33.485 --> 00:02:34.555
Back in the mid 1990s,

00:02:35.115 --> 00:02:36.055
several scientists,

00:02:36.065 --> 00:02:40.125
including doctors James Allison and Tasuku Honjo,

00:02:40.385 --> 00:02:44.935
realized that blocking the ability of the immune cells to see the red light 

00:02:44.935 --> 00:02:49.795
checkpoint on the cancer cells would cause the immune cells to attack the 

00:02:49.795 --> 00:02:50.625
cancer cells.

00:02:51.215 --> 00:02:55.745
Researchers then designed antibodies that recognize and specifically block the 

00:02:55.745 --> 00:03:00.345
proteins that send out the red light signal. With the red light signal 

00:03:00.345 --> 00:03:05.095
suppressed, these checkpoint inhibitor therapies allow immune cells to 

00:03:05.105 --> 00:03:07.215
kill cancer cells more effectively.

00:03:07.945 --> 00:03:10.295
This strategy has worked remarkably well.

00:03:10.665 --> 00:03:15.095
Checkpoint inhibitor therapy was approved by the FDA as a treatment for cancer in 2011, 

00:03:16.375 --> 00:03:21.325
and in 2018, led to the Nobel Prize being awarded to Allison and Honjo for 

00:03:21.325 --> 00:03:22.855
pioneering this approach.

00:03:24.185 --> 00:03:29.075
Another promising cancer immunotherapy strategy has been to enhance the green light on 

00:03:29.295 --> 00:03:29.745
T-cells,

00:03:30.045 --> 00:03:34.755
a method called CAR T Therapy. A patient's T-cells are collected and a 

00:03:34.755 --> 00:03:36.265
new gene is introduced.

00:03:36.585 --> 00:03:41.555
This new gene customizes and reprograms T-cells so that when they are put back 

00:03:41.565 --> 00:03:42.495
into the patient,

00:03:42.505 --> 00:03:46.645
they specifically recognise a particular molecule on the cancer cells. 

00:03:47.345 --> 00:03:52.105
These CAR T-cells seek out and destroy cancer cells that display that molecule. 

00:03:53.045 --> 00:03:57.915
This patient specific customized cancer immunotherapy has also been recently 

00:03:57.915 --> 00:04:00.345
approved by the Food and Drug Administration.

00:04:01.464 --> 00:04:02.774
Despite their success,

00:04:03.124 --> 00:04:05.214
immunotherapies are not without side effects.

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:08.814
Some patients can develop autoimmune diseases and inflammation.

00:04:09.354 --> 00:04:12.114
Cancer cells also have ways of evading immunotherapies.

00:04:13.284 --> 00:04:14.624
In the battle of the cells,

00:04:14.794 --> 00:04:16.564
immune cells can win initially,

00:04:16.834 --> 00:04:17.634
but later on,

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:21.144
the cancer cells can become resistant and the disease may return.

00:04:21.864 --> 00:04:22.564
Because of this,

00:04:22.564 --> 00:04:24.964
scientists and physicians are hard at work,

00:04:24.974 --> 00:04:29.774
trying to find ways to make immunotherapies as safe and effective as possible.

00:04:30.074 --> 00:04:34.744
Perhaps we have just scratched the surface of what our immune system can do. In the 

00:04:34.744 --> 00:04:35.904
battle of the cells,

00:04:35.954 --> 00:04:38.674
perhaps cancer cells might lose out in the end.

